  "image_name": "43a007d5c2af8535dd768bc62b7f395f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a redox flow battery operates and how it degrades during use. In both schematics, a negolyte and posolyte circulate from external tanks through pumps into opposite sides of a cell separated by a membrane, while a load draws electrical energy. The active species on each side are shown cycling between oxidized and reduced forms, with ion transport across the membrane maintaining charge balance.\n\nThe left side emphasizes the intended electrochemical process but also shows one early failure mode: solid material appears to deposit near an electrode, suggesting precipitation or plating that can build up during cycling. The membrane is central, allowing ionic conduction while separating the two electrolytes.\n\nThe right side highlights several parasitic processes that reduce performance. Large arrows indicate crossover of species through the membrane in both directions. Symbols in the tanks suggest self-decomposition of the active materials and electrolyte-side reactions, noted as examples like hydrogen or oxygen evolution. On the negative side, jagged growths represent dendrite formation and electrode passivation. Overall, the figure conveys that redox flow battery performance depends not only on charge-transfer reactions but also on controlling transport, chemical instability, and interfacial degradation.",
